# Probability and Statistics
#
# From Kolmogorov axioms to regression and queues, with a runnable probe battery that checks the famous numbers instead of repeating them.

#>mathematics_probstat
#:layer #!reference
#:domain #!mathematics
#:form #!curriculum
#:level #!foundation
#:review #!adversarial_checked
#:valence #!bone

^"emit"{
 ~#title: "Probability and Statistics"
 ~#claim: "Probability is a measure of total mass one; statistics turns samples into estimates whose error you can state, and every stated error has a model behind it."
 ~#proof: "Kolmogorov, Fisher, and Neyman founded the pieces; Casella and Berger and Wasserman state them; the ASA statement fixes what a p-value means."
 ~#door: "Run the birthday probe first, then read the confidence frame, then the Berkeley case."
 ~#audience: "engineering, economics, and science students, and readers of survey and market reports"
 continuity: "Probability and Statistics | model behind it"
 cold_open: "Every error bar has a model behind it; statistics is the habit of asking which model and whether it fits."
}

